WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:04.419
If a slide needs two headlines, it's two slides.

2
00:00:04.469 --> 00:00:09.076
Here's why one idea per slide is the most useful rule in presentation design.

3
00:00:09.576 --> 00:00:11.465
Here's a typical crowded slide.

4
00:00:11.515 --> 00:00:15.691
A vague headline, and four separate ideas fighting for attention.

5
00:00:15.741 --> 00:00:18.758
Split it, and each point gets its own headline.

6
00:00:19.876 --> 00:00:21.927
The audience knows where to look.

7
00:00:21.977 --> 00:00:24.690
What they see stays in step with what they hear.

8
00:00:24.740 --> 00:00:28.779
And in a video, every new idea means a fresh picture on screen.

9
00:00:29.379 --> 00:00:31.825
It's a default, not a law.

10
00:00:31.875 --> 00:00:39.768
Break it for comparisons, summaries, reference slides and process diagrams, where seeing several things together is the whole point.

11
00:00:40.268 --> 00:00:44.606
Builds let a fuller slide show several items without overwhelming anyone.

12
00:00:44.656 --> 00:00:47.069
Each point arrives as you talk about it.

13
00:00:48.319 --> 00:00:52.924
A deck built on one idea per slide is already most of the way to a good video.

14
00:00:52.974 --> 00:00:59.147
On Pro, a.i. directs it, giving every idea its own animated scene, timed to the narration.

15
00:00:59.197 --> 00:01:01.622
Try it at powerpoint2vid.com.

